+        config::accessibility::SPEECH,
+        "Speech Backend",
+        nullptr,
+        "\"RetroArch\" speaks through RetroArch's own accessibility narrator (NVDA, or the built-in SAPI "
+        "voice). On a RetroArch that supports the accessibility speech extension this preserves the "
+        "reader's interrupt, queue and stop behaviour exactly; on an older one it still speaks, but queued "
+        "lines interrupt and stop does nothing, and the core says so in the log. Nothing extra to install.\n"
+        "\n"
+        "\"Prism\" is optional and needs prism.dll in the melondsds_access folder. It is not required and "
+        "is never loaded or looked for unless you pick it.\n"
+        "\n"
+        "\"Log only\" never speaks and only writes every line to the log.\n"
+        "\n"
+        "No line is ever lost: if the chosen backend refuses one, that exact line is spoken again through "
+        "the RetroArch narrator straight away. A single hiccup does not cost a working screen reader, but "
+        "after three refusals in a row the backend is dropped for the session and you are told once.\n"
+        "\n"
+        "If a backend fails to start the failure is announced out loud too, and speech never goes quietly "
+        "silent. The core asks RetroArch what it can do before every line rather than once at startup, so "
+        "if your screen reader exits, or you switch the narrator off and on again, the very next line "
+        "follows it -- no reload, and it never keeps asking for a queue that is no longer there. With "
+        "\"Prism\" chosen, a screen reader that comes back is picked up within about ten seconds.",
